tics\n\nYou are working entirely inside the current directory. Everything you produce\nmust be written here. The K toolchain (`kompile`, `krun`, `kprove`) and\n`python3` are on PATH.\n\n## Inputs\n\n- `prompt.py` — the exact original HumanEval task prompt. It contains the\n  required entry-point signature, natural-language contract, examples, and any\n  helper code intentionally supplied with the task. It contains no completed\n  implementation of the target function. Do not modify it.\n- `py2mpy.py` — the fixed CPython-AST to K-constructor translator. Do not\n  modify it.\n\nThere is no supplied K semantics in this condition.\n\n## Required work\n\n1. Read `prompt.py`, then create `solution.py` and implement the requested\n   entry point yourself. Preserve the signature. You may reuse helper or setup\n   code that the prompt intentionally supplies.\n2. Keep `solution.py` within the subset accepted by `py2mpy.py` and generate\n   `solution.mpy` with `python3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy`.\n3. Write `semantic.k`, `verification.k`, and `spec.k`.\n4. Exercise the program with `krun`, prove every claim in `spec.k` with\n   `kprove`, and record exact commands in `prove.sh`.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n`solution.py`, `solution.mpy`, `semantic.k` and any helper K files,\n`verification.k`, `spec.k`, and `prove.sh`.\n\nThe container has 8 GB of memory. Keep working while a concrete path remains;\nif the proof cannot close, preserve the best artifacts and explain the blocker\nin `NOTES.md`.\n\nEnd your final message with exactly one line and nothing after it:\nRESULT: <KPROVE_PASSED|PARTIAL|BLOCKED> — <one sentence>\n\nChoose exactly one marker using this ordered decision; stop at the first\nmatching case.\n\n1. `KPROVE_PASSED` iff every required positive target-proof command (commands\n   intended to close the task's proof claims) printed `#Top` and exited 0.\n   Expected-failure mutation probes and other negative validation probes are\n   judged by their expected non-zero result and do not disqualify this marker.\n2. Otherwise, `BLOCKED` iff a concrete hard blocker—such as a missing required\n   input, capability, tool, or semantic support—prevented completion. Name it.\n3. Otherwise, `PARTIAL`: the positive proof condition was not met; preserve and\n   report the best artifacts or progress available.\n\nThis ordered precedence is total and mutually exclusive. Incomplete\ndeliverables or a later Gate status do not create a second marker. This runner\nmarker reports positive proof execution only, not a `VALIDATED` judgment.\nFILE /reference/prompt.py\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle. \n    Otherwise return -1\n    Three sides make a valid triangle when the sum of any two sides is greater \n    than the third side.\n    Example:\n    triangle_area(3, 4, 5) == 6.00\n    triangle_area(1, 2, 10) == -1\n    '''\nFILE /reference/canonical.py\n# canonical.py — the UNMODIFIED HumanEval/71 reference solution (from the dataset).\n# Co-located with solution.py (the proof rewrite) to make the original<->rewrite link\n# explicit & auditable.  entry point: triangle_area\n\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle. \n    Otherwise return -1\n    Three sides make a valid triangle when the sum of any two sides is greater \n    than the third side.\n    Example:\n    triangle_area(3, 4, 5) == 6.00\n    triangle_area(1, 2, 10) == -1\n    '''\n    if a + b <= c or a + c <= b or b + c <= a:\n        return -1 \n    s = (a + b + c)/2    \n    area = (s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)) ** 0.5\n    area = round(area, 2)\n    return area\nFILE /candidate/prompt.py\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle. \n    Otherwise return -1\n    Three sides make a valid triangle when the sum of any two sides is greater \n    than the third side.\n    Example:\n    triangle_area(3, 4, 5) == 6.00\n    triangle_area(1, 2, 10) == -1\n    '''\nFILE /candidate/solution.py\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    if a + b <= c:\n        return -1\n    if a + c <= b:\n        return -1\n    if b + c <= a:\n        return -1\n\n    semiperimeter = (a + b + c) / 2\n    area = (\n        semiperimeter\n        * (semiperimeter - a)\n        * (semiperimeter - b)\n        * (semiperimeter - c)\n    ) ** 0.5\n    return round(area, 2)\nFILE /candidate/solution.mpy\nModule(\n  FuncDef(\"triangle_area\", Params(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"),\n    If(Compare(BinOp(\"+\", Name(\"a\"), Name(\"b\")), CmpOp(\"<=\", Name(\"c\"))),\n      Return(UnaryOp(\"-\", Int(1))),\n      )\n    If(Compare(BinOp(\"+\", Name(\"a\"), Name(\"c\")), CmpOp(\"<=\", Name(\"b\"))),\n      Return(UnaryOp(\"-\", Int(1))),\n      )\n    If(Compare(BinOp(\"+\", Name(\"b\"), Name(\"c\")), CmpOp(\"<=\", Name(\"a\"))),\n      Return(UnaryOp(\"-\", Int(1))),\n      )\n    Assign(\n      Name(\"semiperimeter\"),\n      BinOp(\n        \"/\",\n        BinOp(\"+\", BinOp(\"+\", Name(\"a\"), Name(\"b\")), Name(\"c\")),\n        Int(2)))\n    Assign(\n      Name(\"area\"),\n      BinOp(\n        \"**\",\n        BinOp(\n          \"*\",\n          BinOp(\n            \"*\",\n            BinOp(\n              \"*\",\n              Name(\"semiperimeter\"),\n              BinOp(\"-\", Name(\"semiperimeter\"), Name(\"a\"))),\n            BinOp(\"-\", Name(\"semiperimeter\"), Name(\"b\"))),\n          BinOp(\"-\", Name(\"semiperimeter\"), Name(\"c\"))),\n        Float(0.5)))\n    Return(Call(Name(\"round\"), Name(\"area\"), Int(2)))))\nFILE /candidate/semantic.k\n// A small, executable semantics for exactly the Python-AST constructors used by\n// solution.mpy.  The source translator is purely syntactic; all computation is\n// defined here.\n\nrequires \"rat.md\"\n\nmodule MPY-SYNTAX\n  imports INT-SYNTAX\n  imports FLOAT-SYNTAX\n  imports RAT-SYNTAX\n  imports BOOL-SYNTAX\n  imports STRING-SYNTAX\n\n  syntax Program ::= Module(Stmts)\n  syntax Stmts  ::= List{Stmt, \"\"}\n  syntax Stmt   ::= FuncDef(String, Params, Stmts)\n                  | \"If\" \"(\" Exp \",\" Stmts \",\" \")\"\n                  | Assign(Exp, Exp)\n                  | Return(Exp)\n\n  syntax Params  ::= Params(Strings)\n  syntax Strings ::= List{String, \",\"}\n\n  syntax Exp ::= Int(Int)\n               | Float(Float)\n               | Name(String)\n               | Call(Exp, Exprs)\n  syntax Exp ::= UnaryOp(String, Exp) [strict(2)]\n               | BinOp(String, Exp, Exp) [seqstrict(2, 3)]\n               | Compare(Exp, CmpOp) [strict(1)]\n  syntax CmpOp ::= CmpOp(String, Exp)\n  syntax Exprs ::= List{Exp, \",\"}\n\n  syntax NumValue ::= VInt(Int) | VRat(Rat)\n  syntax Value  ::= NumValue\n                  | VFloat(Float)\n                  | VSqrt(Rat)\n                  | VRounded(Int)\n                  | VBool(Bool)\n  syntax Values ::= List{Value, \",\"}\n  syntax Args   ::= Args(Values)\nendmodule\n\nmodule SEMANTIC\n  imports MPY-SYNTAX\n  imports INT\n  imports RAT\n  imports BOOL\n  imports MAP\n\n  configuration\n    <triangle>\n      <k> launch($PGM:Program, $ARGS:Args) </k>\n      <env> .Map </env>\n      <functions> .Map </functions>\n      <result> NoResult </result>\n    </triangle>\n\n  syntax Result ::= \"NoResult\" | Value\n  syntax Exp ::= Value\n  syntax KResult ::= Value\n\n  syntax Function ::= function(Strings, Stmts)\n  syntax KItem ::= launch(Program, Args)\n                 | exec(Stmts)\n                 | invoke(String, Values)\n                 | bind(Strings, Values)\n                 | branch(Stmts)\n                 | assignTo(String)\n                 | \"returnValue\"\n                 | compareLE(Value)\n                 | \"round2\"\n\n  // Module loading and entry-point invocation.\n  rule launch(Module(SS), Args(VS))\n    => exec(SS) ~> invoke(\"triangle_area\", VS)\n\n  rule exec(.Stmts) => .K\n  rule exec(S:Stmt SS:Stmts) => S ~> exec(SS)\n\n  rule <k> FuncDef(F, Params(PS), BODY) => .K ...</k>\n       <functions> FS => FS [ F <- function(PS, BODY) ] </functions>\n\n  rule <k> invoke(F, VS) => bind(PS, VS) ~> exec(BODY) ...</k>\n       <functions>... F |-> function(PS, BODY) ...</functions>\n\n  rule bind(.Strings, .Values) => .K\n  rule <k> bind((P, PS), (V, VS)) => bind(PS, VS) ...</k>\n       <env> RHO => RHO [ P <- V ] </env>\n\n  // Statements and non-local return from the one active function call.\n  rule If(E, THEN,) => E ~> branch(THEN)\n  rule VBool(true)  ~> branch(THEN) => exec(THEN)\n  rule VBool(false) ~> branch(_) => .K\n\n  rule Assign(Name(X), E) => E ~> assignTo(X)\n  rule <k> V:Value ~> assignTo(X) => .K ...</k>\n       <env> RHO => RHO [ X <- V ] </env>\n\n  rule Return(E) => E ~> returnValue\n  rule <k> V:Value ~> returnValue ~> _REST:K => .K </k>\n       <env> _ => .Map </env>\n       <functions> _ => .Map </functions>\n       <result> NoResult => V </result>\n\n  // Literals, variables, and unary negation.\n  rule Int(I) => VInt(I)\n  rule Float(F) => VFloat(F)\n  rule <k> Name(X) => V ...</k>\n       <env>... X |-> V ...</env>\n\n  rule UnaryOp(\"-\", VInt(I))   => VInt(0 -Int I)\n  rule UnaryOp(\"-\", VRat(R)) => VRat(0 -Rat R)\n\n  // Python numeric promotion for the arithmetic needed by this program.\n  syntax Rat ::= asRat(NumValue) [function, total]\n  rule asRat(VInt(I)) => I\n  rule asRat(VRat(R)) => R\n\n  rule BinOp(\"+\", VInt(I), VInt(J)) => VInt(I +Int J)\n  rule BinOp(\"+\", V:NumValue, W:NumValue)\n    => VRat(asRat(V) +Rat asRat(W))\n    [owise]\n\n  rule BinOp(\"-\", VInt(I), VInt(J)) => VInt(I -Int J)\n  rule BinOp(\"-\", V:NumValue, W:NumValue)\n    => VRat(asRat(V) -Rat asRat(W))\n    [owise]\n\n  rule BinOp(\"*\", VInt(I), VInt(J)) => VInt(I *Int J)\n  rule BinOp(\"*\", V:NumValue, W:NumValue)\n    => VRat(asRat(V) *Rat asRat(W))\n    [owise]\n\n  rule BinOp(\"/\", V:NumValue, W:NumValue)\n    => VRat(asRat(V) /Rat asRat(W))\n  rule BinOp(\"**\", V:NumValue, VFloat(0.5))\n    => VSqrt(asRat(V))\n\n  // The emitted comparisons are single-link chains.  The continuation keeps\n  // Python's left-to-right expression evaluation explicit.\n  rule Compare(V:Value, CmpOp(\"<=\", E)) => E ~> compareLE(V)\n  rule VInt(J) ~> compareLE(VInt(I))\n    => VBool(I <=Int J)\n  rule W:NumValue ~> compareLE(V:NumValue)\n    => VBool(asRat(V) <=Rat asRat(W))\n    [owise]\n\n  // round(sqrt(x), 2), represented exactly as an integer count of hundredths.\n  // The integer/rational algorithm avoids relying on backend floating-point\n  // hooks, so the same semantics is executable and symbolically provable.\n  rule Call(Name(\"round\"), E, Int(2)) => E ~> round2\n  rule VSqrt(R) ~> round2 => VRounded(sqrtHundredths(R))\n  rule VInt(I) ~> round2 => VInt(I)\n\n  syntax Int ::= sqrtHundredths(Rat) [function]\n               | findSqrtUpper(Rat, Int) [function]\n               | bisectSqrt(Rat, Int, Int) [function]\n               | finishSqrtRound(Rat, Int) [function]\n\n  rule sqrtHundredths(R)\n    => findSqrtUpper(R *Rat 10000, 1)\n    [concrete(R), simplification]\n\n  rule findSqrtUpper(R, H)\n    => finishSqrtRound(R, bisectSqrt(R, H /Int 2, H))\n    requires R <Rat (H *Int H)\n  rule findSqrtUpper(R, H)\n    => findSqrtUpper(R, H *Int 2)\n    requires R >=Rat (H *Int H)\n\n  rule bisectSqrt(_, L, H) => L\n    requires H -Int L <=Int 1\n  rule bisectSqrt(R, L, H)\n    => bisectSqrt(R, (L +Int H) /Int 2, H)\n    requires H -Int L >Int 1\n     andBool (((L +Int H) /Int 2) *Int ((L +Int H) /Int 2) <=Rat R)\n  rule bisectSqrt(R, L, H)\n    => bisectSqrt(R, L, (L +Int H) /Int 2)\n    requires H -Int L >Int 1\n     andBool (R <Rat ((L +Int H) /Int 2) *Int ((L +Int H) /Int 2